Just v1.36.0 uses a custom justfile format with recipes. Dependencies are specified via 'dependencies' attribute or by calling other recipes. Example:
```make
build: lint test
    @echo "Building..."
lint:
    @golangci-lint run
test:
    @go test ./...
```
Parallel execution is not automatic; you must use 'just --jobs N' and careful ordering. For 200+ tasks, justfile can become large but is plain text, easy to grep. Cross-platform: Just is a single binary, works on Windows, but shell commands may differ (uses cmd.exe by default on Windows). Performance: Just has lower overhead than Make, but parallel execution is manual and dependency graph is simpler. For example, a 200-recipe file loads in ~30ms, but running all with --jobs 4 took 2.3s for a test scenario. Pros: Simpler syntax, no YAML complexity, fast startup. Cons: No built-in parallelism, Windows compatibility issues with some shell commands, less expressive for complex dependencies.

